## Why This Is Asked
Tests for per-node caches in multi-node services are common in high-scale organizations. A beginner-friendly test plan demonstrates understanding of TTL eviction, cross-node consistency via source-of-truth invalidation, and stampede protection without overengineering.

## Key Concepts
- **Cache TTL and eviction**: Time-based expiration ensures stale data is removed
- **Per-node isolation vs. cross-node consistency**: Each node maintains its own cache; invalidation must propagate to all nodes
- **Cache stampede and coalescing**: Prevents thundering herd when cache misses occur simultaneously
- **Time manipulation in CI**: Essential for testing TTL behavior without waiting in tests
- **Burst-load testing**: Simulates real traffic spikes to verify cache behavior under pressure

## Code Example
```javascript
class Cache {
  constructor(ttlMs) {
    this.ttl = ttlMs;
    this.map = new Map();
  }

  set(k, v) {
    this.map.set(k, { v, exp: Date.now() + this.ttl });
  }

  get(k) {
    const entry = this.map.get(k);
    if (!entry) return null;
    if (Date.now() > entry.exp) {
      this.map.delete(k);
      return null;
    }
    return entry.v;
  }
}```

## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses your understanding of streaming semantics, replay handling, and how to design reliable test plans that remain stable in CI environments.

## Key Concepts

- **Exactly-once processing semantics**: Ensuring each message is processed once and only once, even in failure scenarios
- **Deduplication strategies**: Message IDs, sequence numbers, or content hashing to identify duplicates
- **Replay-safe integration testing**: Verifying that replaying messages does not cause duplicate processing
- **Idempotent sinks**: Designing downstream processors to handle duplicate inputs gracefully
- **Test data design for streaming pipelines**: Creating representative datasets that cover normal cases, edge cases, and failure scenarios

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple in-memory deduplication for messages
function deduplicate(messages) {
  const seen = new Set();
  const unique = [];

  for (const msg of messages) {
    if (!seen.has(msg.id)) {
      seen.add(msg.id);
      unique.push(msg);
    }
  }

  return unique;
}

// Consumer with exactly-once processing
async function processMessage(msg, db) {
  const tx = await db.beginTransaction();

  try {
    // Check if already processed
    const existing = await db.query(
      'SELECT id FROM processed_messages WHERE id = ?',
      [msg.id]
    );

    if (existing.length > 0) {
      await tx.rollback();
      return { status: 'duplicate', skipped: true };
    }

    // Process the message
    await processPayload(msg.payload);

    // Record the message as processed
    await db.execute(
      'INSERT INTO processed_messages (id, processed_at) VALUES (?, NOW())',
      [msg.id]
    );

    await tx.commit();
    return { status: 'processed' };
  } catch (error) {
    await tx.rollback();
    throw error;
  }
}
```

